, "evidence": "以汇通祠为载体系统讲述郭守敬、元代积水潭、大都治水和测天制历，教育叙事强但建筑空间较小。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 46, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "什刹海湖岸景观与生态空间", "HS": 5.8, "IU": 6.2, "VI": 7.5, "VA": 7.3, "CE": 6.8, "ER": 0, "evidence": "西海沿岸湿地提供开阔水面、芦苇和水鸟景观，并连接元代漕运与郭守敬叙事，但自身历史遗产属性较弱。", "confidence": "medium"}, {"seq": 47, "object_type": "object", "peer_group": "什刹海现代人物纪念雕像", "HS": 4.5, "IU": 4, "VI": 5, "VA": 5.3, "CE": 6.2, "ER": 0, "evidence": "雕像主要承担郭守敬及大运河水利功绩的当代纪念和识别功能，作者、年代、材质与尺度均未明确。", "confidence": "medium"}, {"seq": 48, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "什刹海人物纪念馆与故居", "HS": 8, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 7.5, "VA": 7.7, "CE": 8.6, "ER": 0, "evidence": "全国重点文物保护单位级别的真实故居，保留郭沫若晚年生活、写作环境及手稿文献等完整纪念叙事。", "confidence": "high"}]}}
{"cache_key": "a83aacc809de02f516fb9f0728179ebee7c46ffa8e7ce2d3e835b3794cd12821", "provider": "openai_codex", "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "effort": "medium", "stage": "trans_artwork_local_description", "response": {"items": [{"i": 0, "en": "Nanluoguxiang is located between Beijing Drum Tower and Di’anmen, running north–south, with a total length of 786 meters and a width of approximately 8 meters. The accompanying materials state that it took shape during the same period as Dadu of the Yuan dynasty. Sixteen branch lanes line both sides of the street, which retains an overall checkerboard-style neighborhood layout as well as the traditional hutong and courtyard pattern. The materials also record that the lane was formerly called “Luoguoxiang” and was renamed Nanluoguxiang in the 1750 Complete Map of the City. The existing materials describe both the character of the historic district and its later commercial and leisure activities.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 1, "en": "Fuxiang Hutong is one of the eight branch lanes on the west side of Nanluoguxiang, within a traditional hutong area. A 2020 report concerning Dongcheng District included it in the scope of renovation and restoration, stating that the hutong pattern and courtyard character were retained after the work, while residents’ housing, kitchen and bathroom facilities, and municipal infrastructure were improved. The existing materials focus on the overall renewal of the lane and residents’ living environment and do not provide Fuxiang Hutong’s individual length, width, or a specific list of historic buildings.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 2, "en": "Suoyi Hutong is one of the branch lanes on the west side of Nanluoguxiang. A report reproduced by Dongcheng District’s integrated media states that Suoyi Hutong, Yuer Hutong, Mao’er Hutong, and Fuxiang Hutong underwent renovation and improvement together. The project emphasized retaining the original hutong pattern and courtyard character while improving residents’ living conditions and the public environment. The accompanying materials do not provide Suoyi Hutong’s individual historical development, dimensions, or a list of courtyards.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 3, "en": "Yuer Hutong is one of the branch lanes on the west side of Nanluoguxiang. Existing reports record that the hutong’s pattern and courtyard character were retained during renovation and improvement, while residents’ housing, municipal facilities, and the public environment were improved. The reports specifically introduce Courtyard No. 30, formerly the site of a storeroom of the Qing dynasty banner office responsible for annual duty. Some vacated rooms were later converted into spaces for residents’ discussions, public activities, community services, and exhibitions. The materials also state that Qi Baishi once lived at No. 13 Yuer Hutong.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 4, "en": "Mao’er Hutong is located between the Drum Tower and Di’anmen in Beijing’s old city, running east–west, with a total length of 565 meters and a width of 7 meters. Materials from the Capital Window state that it was already called Mao’er Hutong during the Qianlong period of the Qing dynasty. The name has continued to the present; it was temporarily changed to Fourth Huihuang Street before being restored. The hutong contains historic residences and gardens including Keyuan and Courtyards Nos. 35 and 37. Keyuan was formerly the residence and garden of Wenyu, while Courtyards Nos. 35 and 37 once belonged to Wanrong’s maternal family. Existing materials emphasize that the gates of these residences can only be viewed from outside and do not state that their interiors are regularly open.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 5, "en": "Jingyang Hutong is listed in the accompanying Beijing Tourism materials as one of the eight branch lanes on the west side of Nanluoguxiang. Existing sources provide no separate information on the hutong’s length, width, historical development of its name, building inventory, or renovation details. Its identity within the traditional branch-lane system of Nanluoguxiang can therefore be confirmed, but its specific history cannot be supplemented with information about the broader historic district.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 6, "en": "Shajing Hutong is listed in the accompanying Beijing Tourism materials as one of the eight branch lanes on the west side of Nanluoguxiang. Existing sources provide no separate information on its dimensions, changes in name, historic buildings, or renovation. This entry therefore only confirms, on the basis of the sources, that it belongs to the Nanluoguxiang branch-lane system and does not transfer information from the broader historic district or other hutongs.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 7, "en": "Heizhima Hutong is one of the eight branch lanes on the west side of Nanluoguxiang. The accompanying materials also record that No. 2 Heizhima Hutong once housed the “Shaorouren” roast-meat restaurant; No. 59 was formerly the residence of Hong Chengchou, a general who defected to the Qing at the end of the Ming dynasty; and No. 13 was once the residence of Kui Jun, a Minister of the Board of Punishments in the Qing dynasty. These details show that the source presents the hutong as a fragment where traditional streets, historic residences, and commercial activities coexist, but it does not provide the length, width, or uniform access information for the entire hutong.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 8, "en": "Qian Gulouyuan Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the west side of the east-and-west branch-lane system of Nanluoguxiang. The official text provided lists its name in south-to-north order but gives no further information on its historical development, length, buildings, or specific courtyards.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 9, "en": "Chaodou Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the east side of Nanluoguxiang. The official text specifically records that No. 77 Chaodou Hutong was formerly the residence of Prince Senggelinqin of the Qing dynasty, with its front gate on Chaodou Hutong and rear gate on Banchang Hutong. The family ancestral shrine of Senggelinqin was also located at its western entrance. These accounts are historical materials and cannot be used to determine its present condition or access.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 10, "en": "Banchang Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the east side of Nanluoguxiang. The text provided also records that the rear gate of Prince Senggelinqin’s residence in the Qing dynasty was on Banchang Hutong, but gives no further information on the formation, length, street pattern, or surviving buildings of Banchang Hutong itself. This historical association does not mean that the entire hutong consists of remains of the princely residence.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 11, "en": "Dongmianhua Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the east side of Nanluoguxiang. The text records that No. 39 Dongmianhua Hutong is now the Central Academy of Drama and was formerly the residence of Jin Yunpeng. The source also states that part of the Jin residence’s courtyard compound was converted into Western-style buildings and that the old buildings were later completely demolished. These details concern a specific address and do not represent the entire hutong.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 12, "en": "Beibingmasi Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the east side of Nanluoguxiang. The official text provided only lists it in the south-to-north register of hutongs and gives no information specific to it concerning the origin of its name, historical figures, architectural remains, length, or other matters. At present, its identity as a branch lane of Nanluoguxiang can only be confirmed; further individual facts cannot be added on this basis.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 13, "en": "Qinlao Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the east side of Nanluoguxiang. The official text additionally records that No. 35 Qinlao Hutong was once the residence of the Suo family, headed by a Grand Minister of the Imperial Household Department in the Qing dynasty. It does not describe the current condition of the residence or extend the history of this address to the entire hutong. Apart from its place in the name sequence and this historical association, the source provides no further information specific to it.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 14, "en": "Qianyuan’en Temple Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the east side of Nanluoguxiang. Existing official text confirms this name in the register of hutongs but provides no information on its historical development, length, street form, or specific courtyards. The available information is insufficient to determine the hutong’s degree of preservation, current use, or visiting conditions.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 15, "en": "Houyuan’en Temple Hutong is one of the eight hutongs on the east side of Nanluoguxiang. The text records that No. 13 Houyuan’en Temple Hutong is the former residence of Mao Dun, with the rear courtyard arranged according to its original appearance during his lifetime. No. 7 was once Chiang Kai-shek’s headquarters in Beiping; the building was originally the residence of the Qing imperial clansman Zai Fu. These are historical details concerning specific addresses and cannot be generalized to the entire hutong.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 16, "en": "Ju’er Hutong is located east of Nanluoguxiang. The historical development of its name can be traced to “Juer Hutong” in the Ming dynasty; it was later called “Juer Hutong” with a different character, and the present name was established during the Xuantong period of the Qing dynasty. In 1990, Wu Liangyong led the renovation of dilapidated housing and addressed the traditional hutong residential environment through “organic renewal,” allowing it to retain the character of old Beijing courtyard residences while meeting modern residential needs. Public materials also record that the project received the Asian Architecture Gold Award in 1992 and the World Habitat Award in 1993. Ju’er Hutong remains a residential living space, and the materials do not state that courtyard interiors are generally open.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 17, "en": "The Prince Seng Residence, namely Prince Senggelinqin’s residence, is a princely compound extending across Chaodou Hutong, Nanluoguxiang, and Banchang Hutong. The protection area announced by the Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Bureau includes the existing enclosing walls of Courtyards Nos. 75 and 77 Chaodou Hutong, the south-central and north courtyards of No. 110 Nanluoguxiang, and Courtyards Nos. 30, 32, and 34 Banchang Hutong. Another official historical text states that the residence was large, with its front gate on Chaodou Hutong and rear gate on Banchang Hutong. It also records that Senggelinqin died in battle at Caozhou, Shandong, in 1865. After his son inherited the title of Prince, the site was also known popularly as the “Residence of Prince Bo.”", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 18, "en": "According to an official historical cultural-tourism text, the former site of the Senggelinqin Ancestral Shrine was located at the western entrance of Chaodou Hutong and was the ancestral shrine of the Senggelinqin family. The text also states that the shrine and the Prince Seng Residence were built on the same hutong, describing this as an unusual layout in Beijing. Existing materials provide only its historical relationship with the princely compound and its approximate location, without an independent designation number as a protected cultural heritage site, a specific address, information on the preservation of the buildings, or current access arrangements. This entry therefore only identifies the historical site.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 19, "en": "Keyuan and the Wenyu Residence are a connected group of residences and gardens in Mao’er Hutong. The protection area announced by the Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Bureau lists Keyuan as Courtyards Nos. 7, 9, 11, and 13 Mao’er Hutong, and separately lists the Courtyards Nos. 3 and 5 of No. 5 Mao’er Hutong in Dongcheng District. The official introduction states that Nos. 7–15 Mao’er Hutong were formerly the residence and garden of Wenyu, a Grand Secretary in the Qing dynasty, and that Keyuan was modeled on Suzhou’s Humble Administrator’s Garden and Lion Grove Garden. The materials also record that the garden residence later belonged to Feng Guozhang and was used for a period as the Embassy of the Republic of Korea in China.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 20, "en": "Wanrong’s Former Residence is located at Courtyards Nos. 35 and 37 Mao’er Hutong. Official materials state that it was the maternal family home of Wanrong, the last empress of the Qing dynasty, and was commonly known as the “Empress’s Residence.” They also record that Wanrong was born here in 1906. After she was installed as empress, the residence underwent extensive renovation. It later became the home of the scholar Zhu Jiajin and is now a Beijing Municipal Protected Cultural Heritage Site. Public materials specifically state that today the main view is of the exterior of the residence and its gate, and this does not establish that the private courtyards or interiors are open to the public.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 21, "en": "The Qi Baishi Former Residence Memorial Museum is located at Courtyard No. 13 Yuer Hutong, Dongcheng District, Beijing. It is a mid-Qing dynasty courtyard residence, which official materials identify as a protected cultural heritage site of Dongcheng District. In 1955, under the care of Zhou Enlai, the state Ministry of Culture allocated funds to purchase it and made it available to Qi Baishi as a residence. Qi Baishi created a number of important works here in his later years. The memorial museum has been reconstructed according to his living and working conditions in his later years, presenting the courtyard environment and the artist’s residential and creative life. The materials do not provide current access arrangements in this entry.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 22, "en": "Mao Dun’s Former Residence is located at No. 13 Houyuan’en Temple Hutong, Jiaodaokou, Dongcheng District, Beijing, occupying an area of 878 square meters. Mao Dun lived here from 1974 to 1981. The sitting room and reception room in the residence retain their original appearance, and materials including books, daily articles, clothing, stationery, and identification documents from his lifetime are displayed. Public information from the Beijing Municipal Government records that Mao Dun’s Former Residence was announced as a Beijing Municipal Protected Cultural Heritage Site on May 24, 1984. Another official historical text also states that the bedroom and study in the rear courtyard were arranged according to their original appearance during his lifetime.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 23, "en": "The former site of Chiang Kai-shek’s Beiping headquarters is the residence at No. 7 Houyuan’en Temple Hutong. An official historical text states that it was originally the residence of the Qing imperial clansman Zai Fu, with the building oriented north–south and featuring a combination of Chinese and Western characteristics. After the victory of the War of Resistance, the materials record that the residence served as Chiang Kai-shek’s headquarters in Beiping from December 1945 to January 1949. During this period he visited Beiping to express concern for compatriots in the north, and in September 1948 dealt with the Liaoshen Campaign there. Another official historical text states that after 1948 the compound received a new occupant, the “Office of the Central Committee’s North China Bureau.” These are historical uses and do not indicate its current access status.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 24, "en": "A historical cultural-tourism text groups Nos. 3, 5, and 7 Ju’er Hutong with No. 6 Shoubi Hutong as the residence of the father of Ronglu, a Governor-General of Zhili and Minister of War in the Qing dynasty. On this basis, the historical ownership of the residential group to which No. 7 Ju’er Hutong belongs can be identified. Existing sources do not describe the layout of the residence’s original garden, the specific condition of the buildings, or its current use.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 25, "en": "According to a historical cultural-tourism text, No. 35 Qinlao Hutong was once the residence of the Suo family, headed by a Grand Minister of the Imperial Household Department in the Qing dynasty. Existing materials only confirm this historical association with the owner of the residence and provide no information on its architectural development, condition, current use, or interior access.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 26, "en": "According to a historical cultural-tourism text, No. 13 Heizhima Hutong was once the residence of Kui Jun, a Minister of the Board of Punishments in the Qing dynasty. Existing sources provide no further information on the residence’s construction date, layout, state of preservation, or present use. This entry therefore only整理ates the source’s record of its historical owner.", "confidence": "doubt"}, {"i": 27, "en": "The identification of No. 59 Heizhima Hutong as the “Hong Chengchou Residence” comes from a historical account in an old Beijing cultural-tourism text. The text states that it was formerly the residence of Hong Chengchou, a general who defected to the Qing at the end of the Ming dynasty, but does not explain the surviving buildings, changes in ownership, renovation, or access arrangements. This entry therefore only states the historical attribution recorded by the source.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 28, "en": "The courtyard residence at No. 15 Shajing Hutong is located in the Nanluoguxiang area of Dongcheng District. An official tender announcement describes it as a traditional old-Beijing courtyard residence with historic cultural buildings. The compound was listed as a Beijing Municipal Protected Cultural Heritage Site in 2003 and occupies approximately 3,000 square meters. A 2015 announcement indicated that the relevant departments planned to implement lightning-protection works to strengthen management and eliminate safety hazards.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 29, "en": "The New Courtyard Residence Renovation Project at Ju’er Hutong was led by the architect Wu Liangyong in 1990 as a renovation of dilapidated housing. An old cultural-tourism text summarizes it as “organic renewal” of the hutong, adapting it to modern living while retaining the character of old Beijing courtyard residences. Materials from the Dongcheng District government state that the area still retains the character of a traditional residential district of Yuan-dynasty hutong courtyards. Existing materials do not specify the project boundaries, a list of individual buildings, or interior visiting arrangements.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 30, "en": "No. 39 Dongmianhua Hutong is now the Dongcheng Campus of the Central Academy of Drama. An old cultural-tourism text records that it was formerly the residence of Jin Yunpeng and states that after purchasing it, Jin Yunpeng demolished part of the courtyard residences and built several Western-style buildings. The text further clearly records that the old buildings were later completely demolished and that no traces of the Jin residence remain. This object should therefore be understood as a record of a historic site and the development of a former residence.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 31, "en": "Shichahai is an open scenic area in northern Xicheng District, consisting of Qianhai, Houhai, and Xihai, with a water surface of approximately 340,000 square meters. Official historical and gazetteer materials record that its historical names included Bailiantan, Jishuitan, and Haizi. After water-conservancy improvements during the Yuan dynasty, it became an important water area connecting Dadu of the Yuan dynasty with the Beijing–Hangzhou Grand Canal. Cultural-tourism materials further include the scenic spots, historic sites, and residences along its banks within the present-day scenic area and describe it as one of Beijing’s first historic and cultural conservation areas.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 32, "en": "Qianhai is one of the three water areas of Shichahai and forms part of the open scenic area. The materials place it within the historical context of the Gaoliang River water system and the grain-transport system of Dadu of the Yuan dynasty. They record that after the founding of the People’s Republic of China, dredging, embankment construction, green-space creation, and other improvements were carried out. After renovation in the 1980s, lotus flowers were planted in Qianhai, and waterfront features including Tanyuan Shuiyuan, an island pavilion, fountains, and teahouses were developed.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 33, "en": "Houhai is one of the three rear seas of Shichahai and, together with Qianhai and Xihai, forms the waters of Shichahai. An official historical text records that the northern and eastern shores of Houhai were important areas of urban activity from the Yuan dynasty onward. After the founding of the People’s Republic of China, the waters and shores underwent dredging, embankment construction, green-space development, and environmental improvement. After renovation in the 1980s, Houhai, together with the surrounding green spaces and newly built attractions, became a recreational area for waterfront sightseeing, fishing, boating, and other activities.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 34, "en": "Xihai, also known as Jishuitan, is one of the three rear seas of Shichahai. Existing materials discuss it within the history of the Gaoliang River water system, the grain-transport system of Dadu of the Yuan dynasty, and the improvement of Shichahai. An official historical text records that after renovation, the island of Huitong Temple was restored in Xihai; the temple was concealed among rocks, pines, and cypresses, while a two-story box-shaped hall inside the artificial hill was used as a cultural salon, serving functions including sightseeing, cultural entertainment, and traditional local snacks.", "confidence": "official"}]}}
{"cache_key": "5882a73d216f8f5f3bf9184191d1019410d2987eca2831c730eb0b21a316e150", "provider": "openai_codex", "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "effort": "high", "stage": "tier_stage1", "response": {"results": [{"seq": 49, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "近现代名人故居纪念馆", "HS": 8.5, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 8, "VA": 8.5, "CE": 9, "ER": 6.5, "evidence": "兼具清代王府花园遗存与宋庆龄长期居所的双重历史层次，是什刹海连接晚清王府史与现代中国政治文化史的重要节点；历史开放事实明确，但当前开放情况未核实。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 50, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "清代王府及王府遗存", "HS": 8.5, "IU": 8, "VI": 7.5, "VA": 7.5, "CE": 8.5, "ER": 4, "evidence": "这里是溥仪出生地和晚清摄政王府，府邸格局及主要建筑仍具解释力，但西花园已另作宋庆龄故居，其他部分开放情况未核实。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 51, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "清代王府及王府遗存", "HS": 7.5, "IU": 6.5, "VI": 6.5, "VA": 6.5, "CE": 7.5, "ER": 3, "evidence": "庆亲王府保存了较完整的西部生活区和多座厅堂，可说明晚清王府生活，但主要殿堂及东部已有改建且内部参观未核实。", "confidence": "medium"}, {"seq": 52, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "清代王府及王府遗存", "HS": 7, "IU": 6.5, "VI": 6.5, "VA": 6.5, "CE": 7.5, "ER": 3, "evidence": "涛贝勒府兼有清代王府、辅仁大学和近现代校园史，现存中路及东路部分院落仍有风貌，但整体拆改明显且不默认可入内。", "confidence": "medium"}, {"seq": 53, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "近代高等教育旧址", "HS": 7.5, "IU": 7, "VI": 7.5, "VA": 7, "CE": 8, "ER": 3, "evidence": "中西合璧主楼见证辅仁大学的创办及天主教高等教育在北京的发展，建筑尺度和礼堂空间有观赏性，但现为校园建筑、参观安排未核实。", "confidence": "medium"}, {"seq": 54, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "历史商业与社交建筑", "HS": 6.5, "IU": 6, "VI": 6.5, "VA": 7, "CE": 7.5, "ER": 4, "evidence": "会贤堂是北京八大堂之一，临水重楼、花园和戏台能够讲述晚清餐饮社交文化及辅仁大学后续使用，但现状和开放方式未确认。", "confidence": "medium"}, {"seq": 55, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "什刹海宗教与祭祀建筑", "HS": 6.5, "IU": 6, "VI": 6.5, "VA": 6.5, "CE": 7, "ER": 3.5, "evidence": "由醇贤亲王庙转为供奉关羽、岳飞的关岳庙，院落规制和身份变迁具有叙事价值，但年代较晚且当前入内安排未核实。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 56, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "什刹海胡同街巷", "HS": 7.5, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 8, "VA": 8.5, "CE": 8.5, "ER": 9.5, "evidence": "鸦儿胡同保留元明以来的名称沿革和后海沿岸城市肌理，串联广化寺、名人旧居与现代酒吧生活，是体验什刹海胡同环境的代表性公共空间。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 57, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "明清北京城门与城防建筑", "HS": 8.5, "IU": 8, "VI": 8.5, "VA": 8, "CE": 8.5, "ER": 6.5, "evidence": "德胜门箭楼是明代内城城防设施的高完整度实例，体量、绿琉璃屋顶和城门历史均有现场冲击力，但内部开放情况未核实。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 58, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "近现代名人故居纪念馆", "HS": 9, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 7.5, "VA": 8, "CE": 8.5, "ER": 8.5, "evidence": "梅兰芳故居保存四合院空间及缀玉轩，并作为正式纪念馆收藏大量戏曲资料，能集中呈现京剧艺术大师及梅派文化，官方博物馆资料明确记载开放。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 59, "object_type": "site", "peer_group": "清代王府及王府遗存", "HS": 9, "IU": 9.5, "VI": 9.5, "VA": 9.5, "CE": 9.5, "ER": 9.5, "evidence": "恭王府及花园是北京保存最完整且向社会开放的清代王府建筑群之一，兼具和珅、庆王府、恭亲王府历史、府园格局和高密度标志性景观，是本区核心体验。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 60, "object_type": "site", "peer_group": "北京中轴线报时建筑群", "HS": 9, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 9, "VA": 8.5, "CE": 9, "ER": 8.5, "evidence": "钟鼓楼作为元明清都城报时中心延续七百余年，拥有高耸楼体、巨型永乐铜钟和中轴线位置，兼具城市象征性、视觉冲击和都城制度解释力。", "confidence": "high"}]}}
