ce": "清代少见的全封闭式大戏楼，建筑、声学、陈设和王府演剧制度叙事集中，属于恭王府代表性节点。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 70, "object_type": "object", "peer_group": "恭王府碑刻与题刻", "HS": 7.5, "IU": 7.5, "VI": 6.5, "VA": 7.5, "CE": 7.5, "ER": 0, "evidence": "康熙御笔“福”字及其多重吉祥寓意使其成为恭王府最具传播力的文化符号，但碑体尺度和现场可见性有限。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 71, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "恭王府府园建筑节点", "HS": 7.5, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 8.5, "VA": 8.5, "CE": 7.5, "ER": 8, "evidence": "汉白玉洛可可式拱门融合西式装饰与传统题刻，并与大戏楼、后罩楼并列为恭王府“三绝”。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 72, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "恭王府府园建筑节点", "HS": 8, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 8.5, "VA": 8, "CE": 8.5, "ER": 8.5, "evidence": "横贯府园、长约151米的后罩楼规模罕见，兼具王府制度史、建筑规制和室内花园遗迹等多重叙事价值。", "confidence": "high"}]}}
{"cache_key": "ab4521aa1ac83f5ea0d4e4576747821ff25423b727cf31a41c5fb2390bd06173", "provider": "openai_codex", "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "effort": "medium", "stage": "trans_artwork_local_description", "response": {"items": [{"i": 0, "en": "Yinding Bridge is a bridge along Shichahai and is associated with the “Yinding Mountain View” landscape corridor. Official reports state that from the bridge one can look west toward the Xishan peaks, with weeping willows and waterscapes visible nearby; “Yinding Mountain View” is listed as a strategic-level landscape corridor in the core area. The reports also state that lowering the height of the Jishuitan Hospital building restored the existing ridgeline view. Whether the mountains can be seen depends on weather and visibility.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 1, "en": "Desheng Bridge is located at the intersection of Deshengmennei Street, Shichahai Houhai, and Xihai. It is a single-span stone arch bridge combining a sluice and bridge. According to Xicheng District government materials, it was first built during the Ming dynasty, and its name derives from its proximity to Deshengmen. The bridge deck was originally arched; in 1919 it was changed to a relatively flatter deck and a pedestrian walkway was added. In 1943, the stone balustrades were replaced with brick-built parapets in the form of city walls. The above describes historical changes and does not represent current traffic conditions.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 2, "en": "Wanning Bridge (Chengqing Upper Sluice) is located at the intersection of the central axis, the Six Seas, and the Tonghui River of the Yuan dynasty. Sources describe it as part of the former course of the Yu River of the Grand Canal and a Major Historical and Cultural Site Protected at the National Level. The Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Bureau records that the bridge was initially made of wood and later rebuilt in stone; it is a single-span arch bridge approximately 34.6 meters long and 17 meters wide. Chengqing Upper Sluice is located west of the bridge. Six water-suppressing beasts remain on both sides of the bridge, one on the northeastern revetment being explicitly identified as a Yuan-dynasty stone carving. Another source states that it was first built in the twenty-second year of the Zhiyuan era of the Yuan dynasty and records that its original name was restored after reconstruction in 2000.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 3, "en": "The water-suppressing beasts at Wanning Bridge are a surviving group of stone carvings on both sides of the bridge. Materials from the Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Bureau state that there are four west of the bridge and two east of it, and that one on the northeastern revetment is a Yuan-dynasty stone carving. Beijing Tourism Net additionally records the inscription “ninth month of the fourth year of the Zhiyuan era” beneath its jaw. Related sources also preserve folk explanations involving water suppression and foretelling drought, but these accounts are traditional symbolism or former beliefs and cannot be used as descriptions of actual flood-control functions.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 4, "en": "The Lotus Market is located along the western shore of Shichahai Qianhai. Sources state that many lotus flowers were planted in Shichahai during the Ming and Qing periods, especially east of Yinding Bridge in Qianhai. During the Tongzhi reign of the Qing dynasty, merchants were already setting up seats along the shore to sell tea, and the market formally acquired its name in the early Republican period. The market reopened in 1990 and displayed a plaque inscribed by Qigong. Current reports position it as a commercial district combining historical culture, urban memory, dining, daily life, cultural and creative products, and green ecology, but provide no continuing operating commitment for specific shops.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 5, "en": "Yandai Xiejie is located outside Di’anmen Outer Street in Xicheng District. Sources state that it first took shape during the Yuan dynasty; the Qianlong-period work Rixia Jiuwen Kao records its former name as “Drum Tower Slanted Street,” and it was changed to its present name in the late Qing period. The street is approximately 232 meters long and is introduced as one of Beijing’s earliest slanted streets and a commercial pedestrian street with traditional cultural features. A route introduction by the Xicheng District government also mentions folk cultural content within the street, including paper-cutting, sugar figurine blowing, clay figurine making, traditional snacks, and hutong commemorative photography.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 6, "en": "Guangfu Temple is located around Yandai Xiejie and Dashibei Hutong. It was founded by the eunuch Peng in the third year of the Tianshun era of the Ming dynasty (1459), renamed Fuyou Palace during the Qing dynasty, and restored to the name Guangfu Temple during the Republican period. After the founding of the People’s Republic of China, parts of it were converted into residences. The mountain gate, main hall, rear hall, and the main hall within Baiyun Xianyuan remain. It opened after restoration in 2008 and is now jointly used by the Shichahai Subdistrict and the Xicheng District Taoist Association, also serving as the Shichahai Cultural Exhibition Center. Related reports have recorded historical exhibitions and creative activities on the history of Shichahai held there.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 7, "en": "The Imperially Established Temple of the True Lord Huode, commonly known as the Fire God Temple, is located at No. 77 Di’anmen Outer Street. It is a Zhengyi Taoist temple and was listed as a Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Site in 1984. District government materials state that it is traditionally said to have been first built in the sixth year of the Zhenguan era of the Tang dynasty (632), and rebuilt in the sixth year of the Zhizheng era of the Yuan dynasty. During the Wanli period of the Ming dynasty, after a fire in the Forbidden City, it was rebuilt on the Yuan-dynasty temple site and embellished with glazed tiles; further reconstruction took place during the Qing dynasty. The temple faces south, with its mountain gate facing east. The surviving structures include the mountain gate, bell and drum towers, and several halls. After clearance and restoration from 2001 to 2005, it was placed under the management and use of the China Taoist Association and opened to the public.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 8, "en": "Guanghua Temple is located at No. 31 Yao’er Hutong. It was first built during the Yuan dynasty and repeatedly rebuilt during the Chenghua and Wanli periods of the Ming dynasty and the Daoguang, Xianfeng, and Guangxu periods of the Qing dynasty. The temple faces south, with its principal buildings arranged along the central axis, including a spirit wall, mountain-gate hall, Hall of the Heavenly Kings, Mahavira Hall, and Sutra Repository. On the west are courtyards containing the Guanyin Pavilion, Kṣitigarbha Pavilion, abbot’s quarters, Dharma hall, and ancestral hall; two two-story halls remain on the eastern route, while the rest has been demolished or altered. Materials recorded in the Sutra Repository include the Ming edition of the Tripitaka, the Qianlong edition of the Pinqie Canon, and the Japanese edition of the Supplementary Canon. The temple also served at various times as the Jingshi Library, a Buddhist academy, and a primary school. In 1982, the Beijing Buddhist Association was established there.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 9, "en": "The Guo Shoujing Memorial Hall is located within Huitong Shrine on the northern bank of Jishuitan. Related sources state that Huitong Shrine was first built during the Yongle period of the Ming dynasty. From August 2007 to August 2008, the Xicheng District government restored the memorial hall and updated its exhibitions; after renovation, it opened free to the public on September 28, 2008. Cultural heritage bureau materials from 2025 describe it as covering nearly 800 square meters, with a building area of 400 square meters and four exhibition halls focusing respectively on Guo Shoujing’s life, Jishuitan during the Yuan dynasty, water control in Dadu, and astronomical observation and calendar making. Another official report from 2023 states that it has five exhibition halls, with different exhibition names and contents, reflecting exhibition information from different years.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 10, "en": "Xihai Wetland Park is located along the northern shore of Xihai. Sources describe access by a path near the stone memorial archway of Huitong Shrine, with open water, reeds, waterbirds, and other wetland scenery visible. Reports associate the site with the northern terminus wharf of the Beijing–Hangzhou Grand Canal during the Yuan dynasty, noting that Xihai once witnessed the gathering of grain-transport vessels. A statue of Guo Shoujing stands by the shore, and nearby Huitong Shrine has been established as the Guo Shoujing Memorial Hall. Existing materials mainly describe the park’s shore environment, historical water-transport background, and cultural-display association; they do not provide an independent construction date, area, or opening arrangements.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 11, "en": "The statue of Guo Shoujing is located on the northern shore of Xihai in Xihai Wetland Park. Sources describe it as standing by the shore and depicting the Yuan-dynasty hydraulic engineer Guo Shoujing holding a scroll and gazing ahead. Reports associate the statue with Guo Shoujing’s achievement of overseeing the construction of the Beijing–Hangzhou Grand Canal and mention that Huitong Shrine on the northern hillside has been established as the Guo Shoujing Memorial Hall. Existing sources confirm only the statue’s location, depiction, and commemorative significance; they do not state its creator, date of production, material, dimensions, or exact coordinates.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 12, "en": "The Guo Moruo Memorial Hall is located on Qianhai West Street in Xicheng District. It was originally a residence combining Chinese and Western elements. In the late Qing period, it belonged to property associated with Prince Gong’s Residence; in the early Republican period, the Le family’s Daren Hall purchased the land and built the residence. After the founding of the People’s Republic of China, the residence was donated to the state and successively served as the Mongolian embassy in China and the residence of Soong Ching Ling. Guo Moruo moved in in November 1963, living and writing there and receiving members of the cultural community until his death in June 1978. The former residence opened to the public in 1988 and was renamed from “Guo Moruo Former Residence” to “Guo Moruo Memorial Hall” in 1994. Related sources also state that the furnishings from his lifetime, manuscripts, books, documentary materials, and personal belongings are preserved there.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 13, "en": "The Soong Ching Ling Former Residence is located at No. 46 along the northern shore of Houhai in Xicheng District. The Western Garden originally belonged to Prince Chun’s Residence and was historically recorded successively as the garden of Mingzhu’s residence, Heshen’s secondary residence, the garden of Prince Cheng’s Residence, and the garden of Prince Chun Zai Feng’s residence. Beijing municipal government materials state that Soong Ching Ling moved there in April 1963 and lived there until her death on May 29, 1981; it opened to the public on May 29, 1982. Xicheng District materials alternatively state that it was named “Former Residence of Comrade Soong Ching Ling, Honorary President of the People’s Republic of China” in October 1981 and opened to the public the following year.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 14, "en": "Prince Chun’s Residence (Northern Residence) is located at No. 44 along the northern shore of Houhai. It was originally the residence of Mingzhu, became Prince Cheng’s Residence during the Qianlong period, and was transferred to Prince Chun’s Residence in 1888. It was called the “Northern Residence” to distinguish it from the former residence on Baoji Street. The Xuantong Emperor Puyi of the Qing dynasty was born here, and his father Zai Feng was known as the regent. The residence faces south, with the residence compound in the east and a garden in the west. Sources list buildings including Yinan Hall, Baohan Hall, Jiusi Hall, and Siqian Hall. The Western Garden was later converted into the Soong Ching Ling Former Residence.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 15, "en": "Prince Qing’s Residence is located at No. 3 Dingfu Street. It was originally the residence of Qishan, a grand secretary during the Daoguang period, and was later confiscated and bestowed on Yikuang. One source dates the bestowal to the first year of the Xianfeng era (1851), while another gives 1841; regarding the date of rebuilding or ennoblement, the former records rebuilding in the tenth year of the Guangxu era (1884), while the latter records ennoblement as Prince Qing in the twentieth year of the Guangxu era (1894). The buildings are divided into principal halls and eastern and western living areas. Existing materials particularly describe three groups of courtyards and numerous halls in the western section, as well as a number of two-story embroidered towers.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 16, "en": "Prince Tao’s Residence is located at No. 27 Liuyin Street. Prince Tao Zai Tao was the seventh son of Prince Chun Xian Yixuan and the younger brother of the regent Zai Feng. The site was originally the residence of Yinyu, the fifteenth son of the Kangxi Emperor, and was converted into Zai Tao’s residence in the late Qing period. The residence faces south, with the residential compound in the north and a garden in the south. The compound was divided into central, eastern, and western routes; the garden had a covered corridor, pavilions, a flower hall, rockeries, and stables. In 1925 it was rented to the Holy See as the site of Fu Jen University, and part of the residence later became Fu Jen Boys’ Middle School. In 1952, related courtyards were leveled to build teaching buildings and sports grounds; some courtyards on the central and eastern routes still retain the character of a princely residence.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 17, "en": "The Former Site of Fu Jen University (Main Building on Dingfu Street) is located at No. 1 Dingfu Street. The site originally comprised the garden and stables of Prince Tao’s Residence. In 1925, Zai Tao rented it to the Holy See, and Fu Jen University was founded by the American Catholic Benedictines. In 1930, a Chinese-Western-style main teaching building was completed on the vacant land and at the stables. The building once contained classrooms, laboratories, a library, offices, and an auditorium accommodating 1,000 people. In 1952, Fu Jen University merged with Beijing Normal University, and the main building became the premises of the chemistry department of Beijing Normal University.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 18, "en": "The Former Site of Huixian Hall is located at No. 18 along the northern shore of Qianhai and was once one of Beijing’s Eight Great Halls. Its predecessor was the private residence of Bin Ru, a vice minister of the Ministry of Rites during the Guangxu period of the Qing dynasty. In 1890, a native of Jinan, Shandong, opened the Huixian Hall restaurant there. The multi-story building facing the street offered views of Shichahai; the door ornaments bore the inscription “All the Worthies Have Gathered.” The rear courtyard was divided into eastern and western routes and contained multiple rooms, a garden, and a theater stage. It was once a gathering place for literati, officials, and nobles. Sources record that the restaurant ceased operations in the 1940s and was purchased by Fu Jen University in 1948 for use as an alumni building.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 19, "en": "Guanyue Temple is located at No. 149 Gulou West Street. It was originally the temple of Prince Chun Xian and was first built in the seventeenth year of the Guangxu era (1891), completed in the twenty-fifth year of the Guangxu era (1899). The temple faces south. Along the central axis are a spirit wall, central gate, main hall, and rear shrine, with additional structures including a divine kitchen, divine storehouse, sacrificial-animal pavilion, and well pavilion. In 1914, the Beiyang government installed statues of Guan Yu and Yue Fei in the rear shrine, and it consequently also became known as the Martial Temple. After 1949, the site served as the Beijing office of the Dalai Lama of Tibet.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 20, "en": "Yao’er Hutong belongs to Shichahai Subdistrict, Xicheng District. It begins in the southeast at Xiaoshibei Hutong, connects with Yandai Xiejie, and extends northwest to Ganlu Hutong. Its name history includes “Yan’er Hutong” in the Yuan dynasty and “Guanghua Temple Street” in the Ming dynasty; in the Qing dynasty, the names “Ya’er Hutong” and “Yā’er Hutong” developed through phonetic similarity. The hutong is 820 meters long, with Guanghua Temple, the Soong Ching Ling Former Residence, and Xiao Jun’s former residence “Wowo Residence” among the heritage sites and former residences of notable figures along it. Sources also record that after commercial development around Houhai, some houses were converted into bars and the environment and functions of the lanes changed.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 21, "en": "The Deshengmen Arrow Tower is located north of the Deshengmen overpass in Xicheng District and is one of the nine gates of the inner city of Beijing during the Ming and Qing periods. Sources record that it was completed in 1439 and subsequently underwent construction and repair to varying degrees. The arrow tower faces south, has an inverted “凸”-shaped plan, seven bays in width and two bays in depth, and uses a front-tower/rear-extension layout. Its roof is a green-glazed tile roof with double eaves and a hip-and-gable form, edged with glazed tiles. Materials list it as a Ming-Qing city-defense facility and as physical material for studying ancient capital construction, architectural building techniques, and the history of Beijing’s urban development.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 22, "en": "The Mei Lanfang Memorial Hall was originally Mei Lanfang’s former residence and is located at No. 9 Huguosi Street. Earlier texts state that Mei Lanfang moved in in 1950 and lived there until his death; the former residence is a Beijing courtyard house facing south, with a small sitting room, the study “Zhuiyu Studio,” and living quarters. Materials from the Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Bureau state that after Mei Lanfang’s death, his family donated a large quantity of opera materials and documents to the state. The memorial hall began preparations in 1984, officially opened in 1986, and houses related materials. Two official sources differ in their accounts of the period of residence.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 23, "en": "Prince Gong’s Residence and Garden is located at the southwestern corner of Shichahai and consists of a residence compound and a garden. The residence is divided into eastern, central, and western routes, each organized around a central axis with multiple successive courtyard houses. The garden is likewise divided into three routes and contains such features as a Western-style gate, the Stele of the “Fu” Character, Dule Peak, and Bat Pool. Sources state that it was first built in the late Qianlong period of the Qing dynasty and successively served as Heshen’s residence, Prince Qing’s Residence, and Prince Gong’s Residence. Its layout and forms combine official architecture with residential characteristics. The rear covered building and Xijin Studio are both described in different official introductions, but the length of the rear covered building is recorded variously as 156 meters and 189 meters.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 24, "en": "The Beijing Bell and Drum Towers are an ancient architectural complex at the northern end of Beijing’s north-south central axis. The two towers are arranged longitudinally, one behind the other, and once served the timekeeping function of the capital during the Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties. According to sources, the surviving Bell Tower was completed in 1747 during the Qianlong period of the Qing dynasty. It is 47.9 meters high overall, built of brick and stone, and houses a time-telling bronze bell cast during the Yongle period of the Ming dynasty. The Drum Tower is 46.7 meters high overall, originally named Qizheng Tower, and underwent reconstruction during the Yuan and Ming periods as well as rebuilding and repairs during the Qing dynasty. Of the original time drums, only one main drum remains; 25 replica time drums are also displayed.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 25, "en": "Beijing Drum Tower was originally named Qizheng Tower. It was first built in the ninth year of the Zhiyuan era of the Yuan dynasty (1272), rebuilt after a fire, rebuilt again in the eighteenth year of the Yongle era of the Ming dynasty (1420), and reportedly rebuilt again during the Qing dynasty. The surviving building is 46.7 meters high overall and is a timber pavilion with triple eaves and three levels of dripping water. The lower level is a brick-and-stone structure with arched vaults and no beams; the upper level is a brick-and-timber structure. It is five bays wide and three bays deep, with a surrounding corridor. The Drum Tower once served to announce the watches. Of the original 25 time drums, only one main drum remains, while 25 replica time drums are displayed.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 26, "en": "The Beijing Bell Tower is the bell tower within the Bell and Drum Towers complex. Historically, it was first built in the ninth year of the Zhiyuan era of the Yuan dynasty (1272), rebuilt in 1297, rebuilt again in the eighteenth year of the Yongle era of the Ming dynasty (1420), and rebuilt once more during the Qing dynasty. The Capital Window source states that the surviving building was completed in 1747 during the Qianlong period of the Qing dynasty. The Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Bureau records reconstruction in the tenth year of the Qianlong era (1745) and explains that an associated stele records the project in the twelfth year of the Qianlong era (1747). The Bell Tower is 47.9 meters high overall, built of brick and stone, with a double-eaved hip-and-gable roof; its interior contains cross-shaped vaults and a courtyard well.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 27, "en": "The Ming Yongle time-telling bronze bell in the Bell Tower hangs in the upper level of the Beijing Bell Tower. Sources state that it was cast during the Yongle period of the Ming dynasty and bears the inscription “Cast on an auspicious day of the year and month of Yongle.” The bell is 7.02 meters high overall, has a maximum diameter of 3.4 meters, and weighs approximately 63 tons. Sources also record that the Bell Tower uses its courtyard well and cross-shaped vaults to form vertically overlapping resonant chambers, and that historically the bell was struck 108 times at each watch. This bronze bell is a time-telling object within the Bell Tower and is not the Bell Tower building itself.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 28, "en": "The surviving main drum and displayed replica time drums constitute a combination of surviving timekeeping implements and exhibition objects on the second floor of Beijing Drum Tower. Official materials state that the Drum Tower originally had 25 time drums, of which only one main drum survives; 25 replica time drums are currently displayed. Accordingly, the surviving main drum and the other replica drums differ in nature, and all 25 cannot be regarded as Ming-Qing originals. Related records also state that the main drum and group of drums were used to announce the watches at the Drum Tower, with 108 drumbeats sounded each night historically.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 29, "en": "Xiao Jun’s Former Residence “Wowo Residence” is located within the compound at No. 6 Yao’er Hutong and is a small two-story Western-style timber building. Official materials state that Xiao Jun lived there from 1951 until his death. During this period he wrote works including The Mine in May, Historical Stories of the Spring and Autumn Periods of Wu and Yue, and The Third Generation, as well as several collections of letters; he also suffered persecution during the Cultural Revolution. The site is listed as a Beijing Municipal Cultural Heritage Site. An older official text also records that the building had been emptied by early 2005.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 30, "en": "Zhenwu Temple is one component of the “one bridge and two temples” configuration at Desheng Bridge, which together with Desheng Bridge and Yongquan Nunnery forms a historical and cultural landscape along the eastern shore of Xihai in Shichahai. Official materials state that in October 2024 it was included in the third batch of Xicheng District projects for the activation and utilization of heritage buildings. During a site survey in 2025, relevant parties were learning about its conservation and restoration and plans for its activation and utilization. The materials indicate a restoration and planning status and do not state that it is currently open to the public.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 31, "en": "Yongquan Nunnery is part of the “one bridge and two temples” configuration at Desheng Bridge and, together with Zhenwu Temple and Desheng Bridge, forms a historical and cultural landscape along the eastern shore of Xihai in Shichahai. Official materials state that in October 2024 it was included in the third batch of Xicheng District projects for the activation and utilization of heritage buildings. A survey report from 2025 mentions that relevant personnel inspected its conservation and restoration on site and heard about its historical changes, cultural value, and plans for activation and utilization. Existing materials provide no current opening arrangements.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 32, "en": "The Garden of Prince Gong’s Residence, also known as Cuijin Garden, is the garden section of Prince Gong’s Residence and is relatively independent from the residence compound. Official materials state that the garden covers approximately 28,860 square meters and is divided into eastern, central, and western routes. The central route enters through a white-marble Western-style arched stone gate and centers on the Stele of the “Fu” Character inscribed by the Kangxi Emperor. Dule Peak and Bat Pool are in front, while Lütian Xiaoyin and the Bat Hall are behind, forming a richly layered garden layout. The garden’s name is also written in sources as “Langrun Garden or Cuijin Garden.”", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 33, "en": "Yishensuo, namely the Grand Theater, is located on the eastern route of the garden of Prince Gong’s Residence. It was named after the plaque inscribed “Yishensuo” in the Kangxi Emperor’s handwriting that once hung inside. Official materials state that the building faces south, covers 722 square meters, and is a fully enclosed timber structure with three linked volumes and hooked connections. Inside are a stage, audience seating, and boxes in the north. Furnishings include the plaque “Pleasant Things for the Heart,” palace lanterns, Qing-style furniture, and wisteria decoration; its architectural, acoustic, and aesthetic designs each have distinctive characteristics.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 34, "en": "The Stele of the “Fu” Character is located inside Miyun Cave beneath Dicu Rock in the garden of Prince Gong’s Residence. Official materials state that the stele is 129 centimeters long and 54 centimeters wide. At the center of its face is the character “Fu,” with character dimensions of 59 by 33 centimeters; above it is the seal inscription “Treasure of the Kangxi Emperor’s Imperial Brush.” Sources interpret the character’s form as incorporating the images of “子,” “才,” “寿,” and “田,” symbolizing many children, talents, fields, longevity, and blessings.", "confidence": "official"}]}}
