nce": "青石围砌、蝙蝠形池名及榆钱寓意体现王府园林的象征性营造，但历史信息和空间规模相对有限。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 82, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "恭王府花园园林建筑节点", "HS": 6.5, "IU": 6, "VI": 6, "VA": 6, "CE": 7, "ER": 6, "evidence": "御书匾额及咸丰、恭亲王兄弟关系赋予其宫廷家族叙事，但建筑形制和现场体验资料不足。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 83, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "恭王府花园园林建筑节点", "HS": 7.5, "IU": 8, "VI": 8, "VA": 8, "CE": 7.5, "ER": 7.5, "evidence": "花园中路主要厅堂，形制宏整并承载御赐匾额、园游吟咏和恭亲王宴客等核心王府园林功能。", "confidence": "high"}, {"seq": 84, "object_type": "node", "peer_group": "恭王府花园叠石与洞窟节点", "HS": 8.5, "IU": 8.5, "VI": 9, "VA": 9, "CE": 9, "ER": 8.5, "evidence": "大型太湖石假山、制高点、秘云洞及康熙御笔福字碑构成兼具视觉地标和清代皇家园林文化叙事的复合节点。", "confidence": "high"}]}}
{"cache_key": "dbaf2e3fb29dba4a49ca98b926f491b6bfe761fbd9dad439ef2b20a6962e83b8", "provider": "openai_codex", "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "effort": "medium", "stage": "trans_artwork_local_description", "response": {"items": [{"i": 0, "en": "The Western Gate is located at the southernmost end of the central axis of the Garden of Prince Gong’s Mansion and is the main gate of the garden. Official sources state that the gate is built of carved white marble and is a Western Rococo-style carved stone arched gate, incorporating such decorative elements as classical orders, arches, a raised top, shells, and reliefs of Western floral sprays. The outer side of the gate lintel is carved with “静含太古,” and the inner side with “秀挹恒春.” The line-method walls on both sides of the gate also bear finely executed reliefs, demonstrating a combination of Western forms and local cultural decoration.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 1, "en": "The Rear Cover Building is located at the boundary between the residence and garden of Prince Gong’s Mansion and is a two-story building extending from east to west. Official sources state that it is approximately 151 meters long from east to west and 25 meters wide from north to south, with 116 rooms, commonly known as “99 and a half rooms.” Its eastern, central, and western sections are respectively named Zhanji Tower, Fujing Tower, and Baoyue Tower. Passages to the rear garden are located on both sides of the central section. At the westernmost end, there was once an indoor garden called the Waterworks Building, containing flowing springs, rockeries, and pavilions; remains of the indoor garden survive.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 2, "en": "Yin’an Hall is the main hall on the central axis of Prince Gong’s Mansion. It is five bays wide and covered with green glazed tiles. In front are a moon platform, symmetrical stepped side approaches to the east and west, and lamp columns; subsidiary halls stand on both sides of the platform. The official website states that it was first built during the period of the Gurun Princess. The original building and the eastern and western subsidiary halls were destroyed by fire on the night of the Lantern Festival in 1920. The present buildings were reconstructed in 2008 according to the layout of the period of Prince Gong. The original text also records that the hall originally contained the prince’s throne and was used for ceremonies, rituals, governmental affairs, and sacrificial activities at the residence.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 3, "en": "Jiale Hall is located in the fourth courtyard of the central axis of the residence of Prince Gong’s Mansion. It is the bedchamber behind Yin’an Hall and the last main hall on the central axis. The main hall is five bays wide, with a flush-gable roof and a front corridor, and is covered with green glazed tiles; subsidiary halls are located to the east and west. The official website states that it was first built during the period of the Gurun Princess and was originally a place for Shamanist sacrificial ceremonies, hence its other name, “Divine Hall.” The hall once contained a stove, a large cauldron, and a “wanzi kang,” used for cooking sacrificial offerings and distributing the food after the rituals. The eastern subsidiary hall once stored ritual vessels, while the western subsidiary hall once housed sacrificial livestock. Remains related to the rituals are also preserved in the courtyard.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 4, "en": "Baoguang Room is located in the third courtyard of the western section of the residence of Prince Gong’s Mansion. The courtyard is relatively small, with a five-bay main hall and three side rooms on each of the east and west sides. The official website states that it was first built during the period of the Princess’s Residence, and that Heshen once entertained close relatives and friends here. During the period of Prince Gong, the Xianfeng Emperor bestowed the name “Baoguang Room” and explained its meaning as “concealing one’s brilliance and not letting others know,” carrying an admonition for Yixin to restrain his talents and abilities. After Yixin died of illness in 1898, Empress Dowager Cixi and the Guangxu Emperor discussed and settled various funeral arrangements with relevant ministers here.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 5, "en": "Xijin Studio is located in the fourth courtyard of the western section of the residence of Prince Gong’s Mansion and was first built during the period of the Princess’s Residence. The official website states that during the period of Prince Qing it was called Qingyi Hall; during the period of the Junior Prince Gong, Puwei, it was renamed with its present name because it housed the Jin-dynasty Lu Ji’s handwritten copy of the “Pingfu Tie.” The main hall is seven bays wide, with corridors at the front and rear and a hip-and-gable-roofed baoxia at the rear eaves. Inside are a three-bay open hall and three-sided, two-story “immortal towers.” Components of golden-thread nanmu, nanmu partitions, and polychrome painted ceilings were used, and the layout is said to imitate the Palace of Tranquil Longevity. The eastern and western side rooms are respectively Legu Studio and Er’er Studio.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 6, "en": "Duofu Studio is located in the third courtyard of the eastern section of the residence of Prince Gong’s Mansion. The main hall is five bays wide and covered with gray tubular tiles, with side rooms to the east and west. Inside the hall hangs a plaque reading “同德延釐,” bestowed by Empress Dowager Cixi for Prince Gong Yixin’s fiftieth birthday. Square calligraphic panels bearing the characters “福” and “寿” are displayed above the four walls, and a large nanmu bookcase is set against the north wall. The painted ceiling features magnolias and crabapple blossoms. The official website states that remnants of Qianlong-period phoenix-and-seal polychrome painting remain on the beams. A wisteria over one hundred years old stands in the courtyard in front of the hall, and the courtyard is therefore also called the “Wisteria Courtyard.”", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 7, "en": "Ledaotang is located in the fourth courtyard of the eastern section of the residence of Prince Gong’s Mansion. On the ridge purlin of the central bay of the northern roll-roofed section of the main hall are reverse-mounted baofu phoenix-and-seal polychrome paintings; the principal motifs are a spreading-winged golden phoenix, peonies, and auspicious clouds. The official website states that after the Gurun Heke Princess married, she lived in this courtyard for ten years, when it was called Yanxi Hall. In the thirtieth year of the Daoguang reign, after Yixin was granted Prince Qing’s Mansion, he renamed it Ledaotang. “Ledaotang” is said to have been inscribed by the Daoguang Emperor for Yixin before his wedding; he also bestowed a plaque and couplets expressing good wishes. Yixin later used this name for the main halls of several of his residences.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 8, "en": "The Arrow Path is a broad roadway paved with gray bricks between the rear-eave wall of the Rear Cover Building of Prince Gong’s Mansion and the garden. The official website states that it is nearly 200 meters long. In addition to serving as a daily passageway, it was historically used by the prince and his sons and younger male relatives for practicing horseback riding and archery. The sources provided mainly describe its location, material, dimensions, and historical use, without further information on surviving features along the roadway, its construction date, or its architectural structure.", "confidence": "official"}, {"i": 9, "en": "Dule Peak is located in the central section of the garden of Prince Gong’s Mansion, north of the Western Gate. It is an approximately five-meter-high solitary ornamental rock of northern Taihu stone. The official website states that it was placed near the garden entrance both to embellish the landscape and block the view, while also serving as a screen wall and screen. Natural textures formed through years of weathering cover the stone; the original text compares its form to a whirlpool in soft water or thin clouds unfurling. When viewed from below, the two characters “乐峰” can be seen, while the character “独” is hidden at the top of the stone, creating a visual play on its name.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 10, "en": "The Bat Pond is located in the central section of the garden of Prince Gong’s Mansion, north of Dule Peak. It is a pond enclosed with bluestone. Its former name was the “Bat River,” later changed to “Bat Pond”; the name derives from the pond’s resemblance to a bat. Elm trees grow beside the pond. In late spring, elm samaras fall into the pond, and because they resemble copper coins, they were imbued with the auspicious meaning of “an abundance of blessings and wealth,” expressing the wish for both good fortune and prosperity.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 11, "en": "Dihua Studio is located in the central section of the garden of Prince Gong’s Mansion, east of the Bat Pond. It was named after the plaque inscribed by the Xianfeng Emperor in his own hand with “棣华轩.” The official website connects its name with the experiences of Yizhu and Yixin practicing spear and saber techniques in their youth: the Daoguang Emperor bestowed the name “Dihua United Effort” on the spear technique and “Treasure Edge Proclaiming Might” on the saber technique, expressing his hope that the brothers would work together. The sources thus present the courtly familial implications carried by the name Dihua Studio.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 12, "en": "Anshan Hall is located in the central section of the garden of Prince Gong’s Mansion and is a five-bay hall. Its main structure consists of a building with two linked hip-and-gable-roofed sections; a three-bay flush-gable-roofed baoxia is attached to the south, and covered walkways connect the east and west sides. The name derives from a plaque inscribed by the Xianfeng Emperor in his own hand. In terms of historical use, it was originally a place where the owner composed poetry and painted while visiting the garden; during the period of Prince Gong Yixin, it was also used to entertain important guests.", "confidence": "AI"}, {"i": 13, "en": "Dicu Rock is an artificial hill formed by stacking Taihu stones, located north of Anshan Hall in the central section of the garden of Prince Gong’s Mansion. Miyun Cave is concealed within the body of the artificial hill and contains a “福” character stele written by the Kangxi Emperor in his own hand. The top of Dicu Rock is the highest point in the garden, with the “Green Heaven Little Hermitage” and Moon-Inviting Terrace located there. According to the official website, the name is related to moss among the rocks: in the past, after water was stored, emerald-green moss grew on the rock walls in summer and autumn, forming a scene of yellow stone and green moss, lush and dripping with verdure.", "confidence": "AI"}]}}
